# Leaf parsers

The combinators in `parser.lavin` join parsers; the parsers here read text. They are the words a
grammar is spelled with: characters, literal text, names, numbers and quoted strings. Each is
written directly against the input, moving its offset itself, rather than built from smaller
parsers. They run for every character of every text, so they should cost little, and reading a
number one character-parser at a time would allocate a value per digit.

There are two kinds. The **character** parsers, `elem`, `accept` and their relatives, read exactly
the characters they are told to, white space included. The **token** parsers, `literal`, `ident`,
the numbers and `stringLiteral`, skip the grammar's white space first, as `parser.lavin` describes
under Running a parser, and read a token after it.

    module dev.avinlang.parsing

## Characters

The character tests a grammar needs, `isDigit`, `isLetter`, `isLetterOrDigit` and `isWhitespace`,
are methods of `char` in the core library: `elem("digit", c -> c.isDigit())`. This library keeps
none of its own.

The input is UTF-8, so moving past a character means moving past the bytes that encode it, between
one and four.

    // The number of bytes UTF-8 encodes `c` in.
    private width(c: char) -> int = int(c) match
        code < 0x80 -> 1
        code < 0x800 -> 2
        code < 0x10000 -> 3
        _ -> 4

A name is what most programming languages call an identifier: a letter or `_` to begin, then
letters, digits and `_`.

    // Whether `c` may begin a name: a letter or `_`.
    private startsName(c: char) -> bool = c == '_' || c.isLetter()

    // Whether `c` may continue a name: a letter, a digit or `_`.
    private continuesName(c: char) -> bool = c == '_' || c.isLetterOrDigit()

## Helpers for reading

Numbers and strings are made of ASCII punctuation and digits, which can be tested a byte at a time
without decoding a character. `byteIs` does that, and is false at the end of the text rather than
trapping.

    // The byte at `at` as a character: the character itself where it is ASCII.
    private asciiAt(input: Input, at: int) -> char = char(int(input.source.byteAt(at)))

    // Whether the byte at `at` is the ASCII character `c`.
    private byteIs(input: Input, at: int, c: char) -> bool = at < input.length && asciiAt(input, at) == c

    // Moves past the characters `p` accepts.
    private skip(input: Input, p: char -> bool)
        while !input.atEnd()
            val c = input.first()
            if !p(c) then return
            input.offset += width(c)

    // Moves past the ASCII digits, answering how many there were.
    private skipDigits(input: Input) -> int
        val start = input.offset
        while !input.atEnd() && '0' <= asciiAt(input, input.offset) <= '9'
            input.offset += 1
        input.offset - start

The white space every grammar skips unless it says otherwise is white space in any script.

    // White space in any script, none included.
    private lazy val spaces: Parser[unit] = Parser(input ->
        skip(input, c -> c.isWhitespace())
        Some(()))

## One character

`elem(kind, p)` reads one character the test `p` accepts. A test has no name to put in an error, so
the grammar gives one, `kind`: `elem("vowel", c -> "aeiou".contains(s"$c"))`. `acceptIf` is the
same with the message worked out from the character that was refused, and `elem(c)` and
`accept(c)` read the one character `c`.

    // One character `p` accepts, described as `kind` when there is none.
    elem(kind: string, @sendable p: char -> bool) -> Parser[char] = Parser(input ->
        if input.atEnd() then return input.fail(kind)
        val c = input.first()
        if !p(c) then return input.fail(kind)
        input.offset += width(c)
        Some(c))

    // Exactly the character `e`.
    elem(e: char) -> Parser[char] = accept(e)

    // Exactly the character `e`.
    accept(e: char) -> Parser[char]
        val what = s"`$e`"
        val n = width(e)
        Parser(input ->
            if !input.atEnd() && input.first() == e
                input.offset += n
                Some(e)
            else input.fail(what))

    // One character `p` accepts; where it refuses one, a failure expecting `err` of that character, and
    // at the end of the text one expecting `character`.
    acceptIf(@sendable p: char -> bool, @sendable err: char -> string) -> Parser[char] = Parser(input ->
        if input.atEnd() then return input.fail("character")
        val c = input.first()
        if !p(c) then return input.fail(err(c))
        input.offset += width(c)
        Some(c))

`acceptMatch(expected, f)` reads one character `f` is defined at and answers `f` of it, so a
character becomes a value in one step: `acceptMatch("digit", match c if c.isDigit() -> int(c) - 48)`.
`accept(expected, f)` is the same.

    // `f` of one character `f` is defined at, described as `expected` when there is none.
    acceptMatch[U](expected: string, @sendable f: PartialFunction[char, U]) -> Parser[U]
        Parser(input ->
            if input.atEnd() then return input.fail(expected)
            val c = input.first()
            f.lift()(c) match
                Some(u) ->
                    input.offset += width(c)
                    Some(u)
                None -> input.fail(expected))

    // `acceptMatch(expected, f)`.
    accept[U](expected: string, @sendable f: PartialFunction[char, U]) -> Parser[U] =acceptMatch(expected, f)

`acceptSeq` reads the given characters in order, and `accept` of a list is the same. A mismatch is
reported at the character that differs.

    // Exactly the characters of `es`, in order.
    acceptSeq(es: Iterable[char]) -> Parser[List[char]]
        val chars = es.toList()
        Parser(input ->
            for e in chars
                if input.atEnd() || input.first() != e then return input.fail(s"`$e`")
                input.offset += width(e)
            Some(chars))

    // `acceptSeq(es)`.
    accept(es: List[char]) -> Parser[List[char]] = acceptSeq(es)

## Literal text

`literal` reads exactly the text it is given, after white space. What it expects is the text in
backquotes, worked out once when the parser is made rather than on every failure, so an error reads
``expected `]` ``. A literal it reads is noted at the input, so that a `not` just after it that
refuses what follows reports the literal as expected, as `input.lavin` explains.

    // Exactly `s`, after white space.
    literal(s: string) -> Parser[string]
        val what = s"`$s`"
        val n = s.byteLength()
        Parser(input ->
            if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
            val start = input.offset
            if input.source.startsWith(s, start)
                input.offset += n
                input.readToken(start, what)
                Some(s)
            else input.fail(what))

## Names

These are values rather than functions, since they take nothing, and `lazy` so that each is made
once, the first time it is used. Each answers the text it read.

    // A name, after white space: a letter, in any script, or `_`, then letters, digits and `_`.
    lazy val ident: Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        if input.atEnd() || !startsName(input.first()) then return input.fail("identifier")
        val start = input.offset
        skip(input, continuesName)
        Some(input.source.slice(start, input.offset)))

## Numbers

The number parsers answer the text of the number, not its value, so a grammar converts it to the
type it wants: `wholeNumber ^^ (s -> s.toLongOption().getOrElse(0))`. Where a part of a number turns out to have
no digits after it, the offset goes back to where that part began, so the number ends before it.

A whole number is an optional `-` and decimal digits.

    // An optional `-` and decimal digits, after white space.
    lazy val wholeNumber: Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        val start = input.offset
        if byteIs(input, start, '-') then input.offset += 1
        if skipDigits(input) == 0
            input.offset = start
            return input.fail("whole number")
        Some(input.source.slice(start, input.offset)))

A decimal number has digits, a `.` and digits, with one side of the point allowed to be empty: `12`,
`12.`, `12.5` and `.5`, but not `.` alone. It has no sign.

    // Digits with an optional `.` and digits after them, or a `.` and digits, after white space.
    lazy val decimalNumber: Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        val start = input.offset
        if !unsignedDecimal(input) then return input.fail("decimal number")
        Some(input.source.slice(start, input.offset)))

    // Moves past `12`, `12.`, `12.5` or `.5`, answering whether there was one; the offset is unchanged
    // where there was not.
    private unsignedDecimal(input: Input) -> bool
        val start = input.offset
        val whole = skipDigits(input)
        if byteIs(input, input.offset, '.')
            input.offset += 1
            if skipDigits(input) == 0 && whole == 0
                input.offset = start
                return false
            return true
        whole > 0

A floating-point number is a decimal number with an optional `-` before it and an optional exponent
after it: `e` or `E`, an optional sign, and digits. An `e` with no digits after it is not part of the
number. A trailing `f`, `F`, `d` or `D`, which marks the precision of a floating-point literal in
many languages, is part of the text read: `1.5f`, `2e3D`. The text is answered as written, suffix
included, for the grammar to interpret.

    // An optional `-`, a decimal number, an optional exponent and an optional `f`, `F`, `d` or `D`,
    // after white space.
    lazy val floatingPointNumber: Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        val start = input.offset
        if byteIs(input, start, '-') then input.offset += 1
        if !unsignedDecimal(input)
            input.offset = start
            return input.fail("floating-point number")
        if byteIs(input, input.offset, 'e') || byteIs(input, input.offset, 'E')
            val e = input.offset
            input.offset += 1
            if byteIs(input, input.offset, '+') || byteIs(input, input.offset, '-') then input.offset += 1
            if skipDigits(input) == 0 then input.offset = e
        if byteIs(input, input.offset, 'f') || byteIs(input, input.offset, 'F') || byteIs(input, input.offset, 'd') || byteIs(input, input.offset, 'D')
            input.offset += 1
        Some(input.source.slice(start, input.offset)))

## Strings

`stringLiteral` reads a string in double quotes and answers its text as written, quotes and escapes
included, for the grammar to interpret as its language does. The escapes are `\"`, `\'`, `\\`,
`\b`, `\f`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, and `\u` with four hexadecimal digits. A control character must be
escaped, so a raw newline inside quotes is an error, reported as the closing `"` that was wanted
before it. A wrong escape is reported at the character after the `\`.

    // A string in double quotes, after white space, with the escapes `\"`, `\'`, `\\`, `\b`, `\f`, `\n`,
    // `\r`, `\t` and `\u` and four hexadecimal digits, and no control character. The value is the text
    // as written, quotes included.
    lazy val stringLiteral: Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return None
        val start = input.offset
        if !byteIs(input, start, '"') then return input.fail("string")
        input.offset += 1
        loop
            if input.atEnd() then return input.fail("`\"`")
            val c = asciiAt(input, input.offset)
            if c == '"'
                input.offset += 1
                return Some(input.source.slice(start, input.offset))
            if c < ' ' || c == '\u{7f}' then return input.fail("`\"`")
            input.offset += 1
            if c == '\\' then escape(input)?)

    // Moves past the escape after a `\`.
    private escape(input: Input) -> Option[unit]
        if input.atEnd() then return input.fail("escape")
        asciiAt(input, input.offset) match
            '"' | '\'' | '\\' | 'b' | 'f' | 'n' | 'r' | 't' ->
                input.offset += 1
                Some(())
            'u' ->
                input.offset += 1
                for _ in 0..<4
                    if input.atEnd() || !isHexDigit(asciiAt(input, input.offset)) then return input.fail("hexadecimal digit")
                    input.offset += 1
                Some(())
            _ -> input.fail("escape")

    // Whether `c` is an ASCII hexadecimal digit.
    private isHexDigit(c: char) -> bool = c match
        '0'..'9' | 'a'..'f' | 'A'..'F' -> true
        _ -> false
